Log the base address, size and extent of each KV cache backing allocation.

Why: the Xid 31 fault addresses in this stack are page-aligned and recur at a
fixed allocator-relative offset -- a ~57 MiB window at 552-610 MiB into whichever
2 GiB band they land in (handover 38.3). Absolute CUDA virtual addresses cannot be
matched to anything, but `fault_va - allocation_base` can, so the bases have to be
observable. Neither nvidia-smi nor torch.cuda.memory_snapshot() reports these
buffers: vLLM allocates them directly rather than through the torch caching
allocator.

Logging only. No allocation, layout or lifetime is altered, so this cannot change
whether the fault reproduces.

Apply from the installed vllm package directory:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/log-alloc-bases.patch

Written against vLLM 0.27.1.

--- a/v1/worker/gpu_model_runner.py
+++ b/v1/worker/gpu_model_runner.py
@@ -7341,6 +7341,27 @@
             for layer_name in kv_cache_tensor.shared_by:
                 kv_cache_raw_tensors[layer_name] = tensor
 
+        # syv patch: log every distinct backing allocation so a fault address can
+        # be converted to an allocation-relative offset (see
+        # patches/log-alloc-bases.patch). Logging only -- nothing about the
+        # allocation, layout or lifetime is changed, so this cannot alter whether
+        # the fault reproduces.
+        try:
+            _seen = {}
+            for _name, _t in kv_cache_raw_tensors.items():
+                _ptr = _t.data_ptr()
+                if _ptr in _seen:
+                    continue
+                _seen[_ptr] = _name
+                logger.info(
+                    "ALLOC kv_cache base=0x%016x size=%d bytes (%.2f MiB) "
+                    "end=0x%016x first_layer=%s",
+                    _ptr, _t.numel(), _t.numel() / (1024 * 1024),
+                    _ptr + _t.numel(), _name,
+                )
+        except Exception:
+            pass
+
         layer_names = set()
         for group in kv_cache_config.kv_cache_groups:
             for layer_name in group.layer_names:
